  The Chocolatey package's metadata. Rendered by build/make-package-manifests.ps1,
  which substitutes every @TOKEN@; this comment is the template's own and is
  removed when it is rendered.

  The package downloads the release's own signed installer and checks it against
  the SHA-256 the manifest carries - it does not embed the 76 MB installer, which
  is why there is no tools/VERIFICATION.txt: nothing binary is shipped inside it.
-->
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>hmailserver</id>
    <version>@VERSION@</version>
    <packageSourceUrl>https://github.com/Progressiverobot/hmailserver/tree/master/hmailserver/installation/chocolatey</packageSourceUrl>
    <owners>Progressive Robot Ltd</owners>
    <title>hMailServer</title>
    <authors>Christopher Holloway, Progressive Robot Ltd</authors>
    <projectUrl>https://github.com/Progressiverobot/hmailserver</projectUrl>
    <copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Progressive Robot Ltd. Based on hMailServer by Martin Knafve.</copyright>
    <licenseUrl>https://github.com/Progressiverobot/hmailserver/blob/master/LICENSE</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <projectSourceUrl>https://github.com/Progressiverobot/hmailserver</projectSourceUrl>
    <docsUrl>https://github.com/Progressiverobot/hmailserver/wiki</docsUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>https://github.com/Progressiverobot/hmailserver/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <tags>hmailserver mail mailserver email smtp imap pop3 dkim dmarc spam webmail server admin</tags>
    <summary>A free, open-source mail server for Windows - SMTP, IMAP and POP3, with anti-spam and anti-virus filtering, DKIM, DMARC, MTA-STS, a webmail client and a REST API.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
hMailServer is a free, open-source mail server implementing SMTP, IMAP and POP3, with a Windows
administration program, a browser administration console, a webmail client and a REST API. This is
the maintained fork of the original project, which is no longer developed upstream: a current
toolchain, current cryptography, and the transport-security and authentication standards a mail
server is expected to speak in 2026 - while remaining a drop-in upgrade for an existing hMailServer
installation, with a continuous database upgrade chain from every earlier version.

### What it does

SMTP with submission and implicit TLS, IMAP4rev1 with the extensions a modern client asks for, POP3,
ManageSieve, SPF, DKIM signing and verification, DMARC with reporting, MTA-STS and TLS-RPT, DANE,
ARC, SpamAssassin and ClamAV integration, greylisting, DNS blacklists and SURBL, auto-ban,
two-factor authentication for administrators and accounts, application passwords, OAuth 2.0
(inbound and outbound, including Microsoft 365), CardDAV address books, full-text search,
quarantine, archiving and legal holds, backup and restore, Prometheus metrics and OpenTelemetry
export.

### What this package does

It downloads the release's Authenticode-signed installer from GitHub, checks it against the SHA-256
recorded in this package, and runs it unattended. That installs the server, the Control Panel and
the setup tools, and configures the embedded database.

An unattended install leaves the administrator password unset, because the wizard page that asks
for one is skipped. Set one before the server is reachable from anywhere but the machine itself:
open the Control Panel, or run `"C:\Program Files\hMailServer\Bin\hMailServer.exe" --set-admin-password`.

Uninstalling runs the installer's own uninstaller. It does not delete the message store or the
database: that is deliberate, and removing them is the administrator's decision to make by hand.
]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/Progressiverobot/hmailserver/releases/tag/@TAG@</releaseNotes>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
